1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,692
On the western edge of San Francisco

2
00:00:02,692 --> 00:00:08,077
there is a four lane highway that
the city is tearing out on purpose.

3
00:00:08,077 --> 00:00:10,000
It runs along Ocean Beach,

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,769
past rows of pastel houses that sell for
two, three, and sometimes four million

5
00:00:15,769 --> 00:00:19,615
The bulldozers are not coming
because the road wore out.

6
00:00:19,615 --> 00:00:22,308
They are coming because the city decided

7
00:00:22,308 --> 00:00:25,385
the Pacific Ocean had
already won the argument.

8
00:00:25,385 --> 00:00:29,231
So why would a city
give up its own coastal

9
00:00:29,231 --> 00:00:33,077
road while the houses behind
it keep getting more expensive?

10
00:00:33,677 --> 00:00:35,600
The road opened in 1929.

11
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:39,446
In the same years the city
poured a concrete seawall

12
00:00:39,446 --> 00:00:43,677
along the beach and built a
boardwalk lined with amusement rides.

13
00:00:43,677 --> 00:00:46,754
Back then the sand dunes
still rolled unbroken

14
00:00:46,754 --> 00:00:49,831
from the beach almost to Golden Gate Park.

15
00:00:49,831 --> 00:00:53,292
The road was meant to be a scenic drive.

16
00:00:53,292 --> 00:00:56,754
Families took a Sunday
car out to the water

17
00:00:56,754 --> 00:01:00,215
and watched the fog come in over the hood.

18
00:01:00,215 --> 00:01:04,446
For nearly a hundred years
that is exactly what it was.

19
00:01:04,446 --> 00:01:08,677
Today the southern stretch is closed
to cars behind orange barriers.

20
00:01:08,677 --> 00:01:12,138
The middle stretch has
been turned into a park.

21
00:01:12,138 --> 00:01:15,215
And the sand keeps
arriving on the pavement

22
00:01:15,215 --> 00:01:18,292
faster than the plows can push it back.

23
00:01:18,292 --> 00:01:22,908
The homeowners along the road
did not vote for most of this.

24
00:01:22,908 --> 00:01:25,985
Yet the value of their houses is tied

25
00:01:25,985 --> 00:01:30,215
to a strip of asphalt that
no longer has a future.

26
00:01:30,815 --> 00:01:33,892
To understand how a city loses a highway,

27
00:01:33,892 --> 00:01:36,585
you have to start with the sand.

28
00:01:36,585 --> 00:01:40,431
Ocean Beach is roughly
three and a half miles long.

29
00:01:40,431 --> 00:01:43,508
It is the widest beach in San Francisco

30
00:01:43,508 --> 00:01:47,354
and the only one that faces
the open Pacific directly.

31
00:01:47,354 --> 00:01:51,585
The waves here are born in
storms thousands of miles away.

32
00:01:51,585 --> 00:01:55,046
In winter they break at
fifteen and twenty feet,

33
00:01:55,046 --> 00:02:00,431
and the surfers who paddle out are
among the most experienced in the state.

34
00:02:00,431 --> 00:02:03,123
The wind blows onshore
almost every afternoon.

35
00:02:03,123 --> 00:02:06,585
It carries fine dune
sand east across the road

36
00:02:06,585 --> 00:02:10,046
in drifts that can
close a lane by morning.

37
00:02:10,046 --> 00:02:12,354
City crews have plowed that sand

38
00:02:12,354 --> 00:02:15,431
for decades the way
northern towns plow snow.

39
00:02:15,431 --> 00:02:18,123
Front loaders scoop it off the lanes

40
00:02:18,123 --> 00:02:21,585
and dump trucks carry
it back to the beach.

41
00:02:21,585 --> 00:02:26,585
On the worst days the road closed
entirely until the sand was cleared.

42
00:02:26,585 --> 00:02:30,046
Anyone who has driven
the road in a westerly

43
00:02:30,046 --> 00:02:33,508
gale remembers the sound of
grit against the windshield.

44
00:02:33,508 --> 00:02:35,815
That sand is the beach moving.

45
00:02:35,815 --> 00:02:39,277
It shifts north in summer
and south in winter.

46
00:02:39,277 --> 00:02:42,354
Over the long run it has been leaving

47
00:02:42,354 --> 00:02:46,585
the southern end of the beach
faster than it comes back.

48
00:02:47,185 --> 00:02:51,800
The southern end is where the
story of the road actually begins.

49
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:56,415
South of Sloat Boulevard the highway
does not sit behind a seawall.

50
00:02:56,415 --> 00:02:59,108
It sits on top of a sandy

51
00:02:59,108 --> 00:03:01,800
bluff about forty feet above the water.

52
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:06,800
The bluff is made of old dune sand
and decades of construction fill.

53
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:11,415
Beneath it runs one of the most
important pipes in the city.

54
00:03:11,415 --> 00:03:16,031
The Lake Merced Tunnel is a concrete
sewer line fourteen feet across.

55
00:03:16,031 --> 00:03:19,492
It carries stormwater and
wastewater from the west side

56
00:03:19,492 --> 00:03:23,723
of San Francisco to the Oceanside
treatment plant beside the zoo.

57
00:03:23,723 --> 00:03:27,954
When it rains hard, that
tunnel is what keeps sewage out

58
00:03:27,954 --> 00:03:32,185
of the ocean and off the
streets of the Sunset District.

59
00:03:32,185 --> 00:03:37,185
The plant next to it handles a large
share of the city's wastewater.

60
00:03:37,185 --> 00:03:42,185
So the question in front of the
city was bigger than a road.

61
00:03:42,185 --> 00:03:47,954
It was about whether a forty foot pile
of sand could keep protecting a tunnel,

62
00:03:47,954 --> 00:03:50,646
a plant, and a neighborhood behind them.

63
00:03:51,246 --> 00:03:55,092
The winter that answered the
question came in early 2010.

64
00:03:55,092 --> 00:03:58,169
A strong El Nino season sent one storm

65
00:03:58,169 --> 00:04:01,246
after another into the
coast of central California.

66
00:04:01,246 --> 00:04:06,246
At Ocean Beach the waves cut straight
into the bluff south of Sloat.

67
00:04:06,246 --> 00:04:08,938
In a matter of weeks the edge

68
00:04:08,938 --> 00:04:13,169
of the cliff moved back by
roughly forty feet in places.

69
00:04:13,169 --> 00:04:15,477
Sections of the parking lots along

70
00:04:15,477 --> 00:04:18,938
the highway broke off and
dropped onto the sand.

71
00:04:18,938 --> 00:04:22,015
The southbound lanes were
closed as the pavement

72
00:04:22,015 --> 00:04:25,092
cracked within a few yards of the drop.

73
00:04:25,092 --> 00:04:27,400
City engineers stood at the edge

74
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:30,862
and looked at the raw face of the bluff.

75
00:04:30,862 --> 00:04:35,477
The sewer tunnel was closer to
daylight than anyone had planned for.

76
00:04:36,077 --> 00:04:38,769
What happened next is the same move

77
00:04:38,769 --> 00:04:41,846
you see on every eroding
coastline in California.

78
00:04:41,846 --> 00:04:43,385
Trucks brought in boulders.

79
00:04:43,385 --> 00:04:47,231
Thousands of tons of rock
were pushed over the edge

80
00:04:47,231 --> 00:04:51,846
and stacked against the base of the
bluff as an emergency revetment.

81
00:04:51,846 --> 00:04:54,538
Old concrete rubble that had been buried

82
00:04:54,538 --> 00:04:58,385
in the fill for decades
tumbled out onto the beach.

83
00:04:58,385 --> 00:05:00,692
Chunks of curb and slab lay

84
00:05:00,692 --> 00:05:04,538
in the surf with rusted
rebar sticking out of them.

85
00:05:04,538 --> 00:05:09,154
Surfers and dog walkers picked their
way around the debris for years.

86
00:05:09,154 --> 00:05:13,385
The California Coastal Commission regulates
almost everything built along the shoreline.

87
00:05:13,385 --> 00:05:16,462
It allowed the rock as
a temporary measure.

88
00:05:16,462 --> 00:05:21,462
Temporary is a word that does
a lot of work on this coast.

89
00:05:21,462 --> 00:05:25,308
Fifteen years later much of
that rock is still there.

90
00:05:25,908 --> 00:05:27,831
The rock protected the tunnel,

91
00:05:27,831 --> 00:05:32,062
but it did nothing for
the beach in front of it.

92
00:05:32,062 --> 00:05:35,908
Along a hard revetment the
sand tends to scour away.

93
00:05:35,908 --> 00:05:38,985
At high tide the water
reaches the boulders

94
00:05:38,985 --> 00:05:42,062
with no dry sand left to walk on.

95
00:05:42,062 --> 00:05:45,138
The structure that saved
the road was slowly

96
00:05:45,138 --> 00:05:48,600
erasing the beach the
road was built to enjoy.

97
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:52,062
For a city that sells
itself on its coastline,

98
00:05:52,062 --> 00:05:54,369
that trade looked worse every winter.

99
00:05:54,369 --> 00:05:58,985
Photographs from the years after 2010
show tumbled rock and broken asphalt.

100
00:05:58,985 --> 00:06:03,600
A chain link fence stands
where a parking lot used to be.

101
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:07,277
The storms did not stop after 2010 either.

102
00:06:07,277 --> 00:06:10,354
In January 2023 a string
of atmospheric river

103
00:06:10,354 --> 00:06:13,431
storms hit the California
coast for three weeks.

104
00:06:13,431 --> 00:06:16,892
At Sloat the waves once
again reached the base

105
00:06:16,892 --> 00:06:21,123
of the bluff and pulled sand
out from under the revetment.

106
00:06:21,123 --> 00:06:24,585
Crews came back with more
rock and more trucks.

107
00:06:24,585 --> 00:06:26,892
Each repair bought another few winters.

108
00:06:26,892 --> 00:06:29,585
Nobody involved described
it as a solution.

109
00:06:30,185 --> 00:06:35,185
In 2012 a planning organization called
SPUR published the Ocean Beach Master Plan.

110
00:06:35,185 --> 00:06:39,031
It was not a law, only a
set of recommendations.

111
00:06:39,031 --> 00:06:42,492
But it laid out an idea that almost no

112
00:06:42,492 --> 00:06:45,954
American city had been
willing to say out loud.

113
00:06:45,954 --> 00:06:50,185
South of Sloat, the plan said,
the city should stop fighting.

114
00:06:50,185 --> 00:06:52,492
Remove the highway from that stretch.

115
00:06:52,492 --> 00:06:55,185
Reroute traffic onto Sloat
and Skyline boulevards.

116
00:06:55,185 --> 00:06:58,646
Protect the sewer tunnel
with a low wall buried

117
00:06:58,646 --> 00:07:04,031
under a restored beach instead of a
pile of boulders standing in the surf.

118
00:07:04,031 --> 00:07:05,954
Planners call this managed retreat.

119
00:07:05,954 --> 00:07:09,800
Residents along the coast usually
call it something less polite.

120
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:13,477
The city adopted the core of that idea.

121
00:07:13,477 --> 00:07:16,938
Under the name Ocean Beach
Climate Change Adaptation Project,

122
00:07:16,938 --> 00:07:20,785
San Francisco committed to removing
the roadway south of Sloat.

123
00:07:20,785 --> 00:07:25,785
The plan calls for a buried seawall
to shield the Lake Merced Tunnel.

124
00:07:25,785 --> 00:07:28,477
New sand goes on top of it,

125
00:07:28,477 --> 00:07:32,323
and a coastal trail runs
where the cars used to.

126
00:07:32,323 --> 00:07:35,015
It moved through years
of environmental review

127
00:07:35,015 --> 00:07:38,862
and won its Coastal Commission
approval in the early 2020s.

128
00:07:38,862 --> 00:07:43,092
Construction was expected to run
through the middle of the decade.

129
00:07:43,092 --> 00:07:45,400
Cost estimates have shifted several times.

130
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:49,631
I have not found a single
final figure I would trust,

131
00:07:49,631 --> 00:07:53,477
but every version runs well past
one hundred million dollars.

132
00:07:53,477 --> 00:07:57,708
The southern lanes were closed to
traffic ahead of the demolition,

133
00:07:57,708 --> 00:07:59,631
and the barriers went up.

134
00:08:00,231 --> 00:08:04,462
That was the part of the
road the ocean took directly.

135
00:08:04,462 --> 00:08:07,538
The next part was taken by a ballot.

136
00:08:08,138 --> 00:08:10,446
Between Sloat Boulevard and Lincoln Way,

137
00:08:10,446 --> 00:08:15,062
the upper highway runs about two
miles behind the old 1929 seawall.

138
00:08:15,062 --> 00:08:19,292
Here the problem is sand piling
up rather than sand disappearing,

139
00:08:19,292 --> 00:08:21,985
and the road itself was structurally fine.

140
00:08:21,985 --> 00:08:25,062
In the spring of 2020,
during the pandemic,

141
00:08:25,062 --> 00:08:29,677
the city closed it to cars so
people had somewhere to walk.

142
00:08:29,677 --> 00:08:34,292
Joggers, cyclists, and parents with
strollers filled four lanes of empty

143
00:08:34,292 --> 00:08:38,138
The closure was popular with
families from across the city.

144
00:08:38,138 --> 00:08:42,369
It was deeply unpopular with
many of the people who actually

145
00:08:42,369 --> 00:08:46,985
lived in the Sunset and used
the road to get to work.

146
00:08:46,985 --> 00:08:49,677
For four years the city ran compromises.

147
00:08:49,677 --> 00:08:51,985
Cars on weekdays, pedestrians on weekends.

148
00:08:51,985 --> 00:08:54,677
Then in November 2024 the question went

149
00:08:54,677 --> 00:08:58,138
to every voter in San
Francisco as Proposition K.

150
00:08:58,738 --> 00:09:01,815
Proposition K asked whether
the upper highway should

151
00:09:01,815 --> 00:09:06,046
be closed to private vehicles
permanently and converted into a park.

152
00:09:06,046 --> 00:09:09,892
It passed with roughly fifty
five percent of the vote.

153
00:09:09,892 --> 00:09:14,508
The map of that vote tells the
whole story of the neighborhood.

154
00:09:14,508 --> 00:09:17,200
Precincts in the Sunset
and Richmond districts,

155
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:21,046
the neighborhoods closest to the
beach, voted heavily against it.

156
00:09:21,046 --> 00:09:24,508
The eastern side of the
city carried the measure.

157
00:09:24,508 --> 00:09:27,969
Those are residents who
visit the beach on weekends

158
00:09:27,969 --> 00:09:31,815
and never sit in the commute
traffic on Nineteenth Avenue.

159
00:09:31,815 --> 00:09:36,046
In April 2025 the city opened
the stretch as Sunset Dunes.

160
00:09:36,046 --> 00:09:40,277
It is a linear park with
painted murals on the asphalt,

161
00:09:40,277 --> 00:09:43,354
a skate spot, and
benches facing the water.

162
00:09:43,354 --> 00:09:46,431
On a weekend afternoon
thousands of people walk,

163
00:09:46,431 --> 00:09:49,123
roll, and ride along the old lanes.

164
00:09:49,123 --> 00:09:51,431
Food trucks park at the entrances.

165
00:09:51,431 --> 00:09:53,738
For the people who use it,

166
00:09:53,738 --> 00:09:59,123
the park is one of the best things
the city has built in years.

167
00:09:59,723 --> 00:10:04,723
For the people who live on the row,
the picture is more complicated.

168
00:10:04,723 --> 00:10:08,954
Their driveways now open onto
a park instead of a road.

169
00:10:08,954 --> 00:10:11,646
Deliveries, contractors, and
moving trucks squeeze onto

170
00:10:11,646 --> 00:10:14,338
the narrow side streets behind the houses.

171
00:10:14,338 --> 00:10:17,800
Emergency vehicles have a
longer route to the beach.

172
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:23,569
And the plows that once kept the sand
off the pavement come far less often,

173
00:10:23,569 --> 00:10:26,262
because there are no cars to protect.

174
00:10:26,862 --> 00:10:29,938
The political bill came
due five months later.

175
00:10:29,938 --> 00:10:34,169
Joel Engardio was the district supervisor
who had championed the measure.

176
00:10:34,169 --> 00:10:37,246
In September 2025 he
faced a recall election

177
00:10:37,246 --> 00:10:40,708
driven largely by residents
angry about losing the road.

178
00:10:40,708 --> 00:10:42,631
He was removed from office.

179
00:10:42,631 --> 00:10:47,631
A supervisor recalled over a highway
closure is rare anywhere in the country.

180
00:10:47,631 --> 00:10:50,708
It shows how much that strip of pavement

181
00:10:50,708 --> 00:10:53,785
meant to the people who lived along it.

182
00:10:53,785 --> 00:10:57,631
Traffic on the parallel streets,
Sunset Boulevard and Nineteenth Avenue,

183
00:10:57,631 --> 00:11:00,323
became a daily argument
at neighborhood meetings.

184
00:11:00,323 --> 00:11:02,631
Whatever you think of the outcome,

185
00:11:02,631 --> 00:11:07,246
the residents were right that something
had been taken away from them.

186
00:11:07,846 --> 00:11:10,538
Now walk the length of the road

187
00:11:10,538 --> 00:11:14,000
as it stands today and look at the houses.

188
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,308
That is where the money is.

189
00:11:16,308 --> 00:11:19,385
The row of homes directly
facing the highway

190
00:11:19,385 --> 00:11:22,846
was mostly built between
the 1920s and the 1950s.

191
00:11:22,846 --> 00:11:26,692
They are two and three story
stucco houses with garages

192
00:11:26,692 --> 00:11:30,923
on the ground floor and living
rooms upstairs for the view.

193
00:11:30,923 --> 00:11:35,154
In the Outer Sunset the median
home has been selling around

194
00:11:35,154 --> 00:11:39,385
one and a half to two million
dollars in recent years.

195
00:11:39,385 --> 00:11:42,462
The oceanfront row along
the highway commands more,

196
00:11:42,462 --> 00:11:45,923
and individual sales there have
cleared three million dollars.

197
00:11:45,923 --> 00:11:50,538
Real estate listings describe the location
with words like unobstructed and forever.

198
00:11:50,538 --> 00:11:54,385
The photographs are always taken
on the rare clear afternoon,

199
00:11:54,385 --> 00:11:59,000
with the sun setting into the
water and the fog held offshore.

200
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:03,615
Buyers from the tech companies downtown
have paid cash for these houses.

201
00:12:03,615 --> 00:12:06,308
Some of them moved in without ever

202
00:12:06,308 --> 00:12:09,000
spending a winter week on the block.

203
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,692
On paper these are among the most

204
00:12:11,692 --> 00:12:14,385
desirable addresses in
a very expensive city.

205
00:12:14,385 --> 00:12:17,462
In practice their owners deal with a set

206
00:12:17,462 --> 00:12:21,308
of problems that do not show
up in listing photographs.

207
00:12:21,908 --> 00:12:23,831
The first problem is sand.

208
00:12:23,831 --> 00:12:28,446
Every westerly wind moves the dunes
in front of the seawall eastward.

209
00:12:28,446 --> 00:12:31,523
The road was the buffer that caught it.

210
00:12:31,523 --> 00:12:34,600
With the cars gone, the city no longer

211
00:12:34,600 --> 00:12:38,062
plows the upper highway
the way it used to.

212
00:12:38,062 --> 00:12:43,062
The drifts creep across the old lanes
toward the sidewalks and garage doors.

213
00:12:43,062 --> 00:12:45,754
Homeowners along the row sweep sand off

214
00:12:45,754 --> 00:12:49,215
their front steps the way
other people sweep leaves.

215
00:12:49,215 --> 00:12:52,677
Salt spray pits the paint
on cars parked outside.

216
00:12:52,677 --> 00:12:56,908
It corrodes window frames and
door hinges within a few years.

217
00:12:56,908 --> 00:13:00,754
Fog sits over the neighborhood
for much of the summer.

218
00:13:00,754 --> 00:13:03,831
The sunshine that the
rest of California takes

219
00:13:03,831 --> 00:13:08,062
for granted often does not appear
on this block until afternoon.

220
00:13:08,662 --> 00:13:10,585
The second problem is water.

221
00:13:10,585 --> 00:13:13,277
Over the life of a thirty year

222
00:13:13,277 --> 00:13:16,354
mortgage it is the one that matters most.

223
00:13:16,354 --> 00:13:18,277
The 1929 seawall is aging.

224
00:13:18,277 --> 00:13:20,585
It was designed under
Michael O'Shaughnessy,

225
00:13:20,585 --> 00:13:24,815
the city engineer who also built
the Hetch Hetchy water system.

226
00:13:24,815 --> 00:13:27,508
His crews poured a curved concrete wall

227
00:13:27,508 --> 00:13:30,585
with a wide stairway
running along its top.

228
00:13:30,585 --> 00:13:34,815
The wall was built to keep
sand off a scenic road.

229
00:13:34,815 --> 00:13:39,046
It was never designed as a
defense against a rising ocean,

230
00:13:39,046 --> 00:13:43,662
and it has been repaired, buried,
and dug out many times since.

231
00:13:43,662 --> 00:13:50,969
Walk the beach at low tide and you can see
where the concrete has been patched in

232
00:13:50,969 --> 00:13:53,277
each repair a slightly different color.

233
00:13:53,277 --> 00:13:57,892
California's official planning guidance
tells cities to prepare for sea levels

234
00:13:57,892 --> 00:14:02,508
roughly one and three feet higher
by the end of this century.

235
00:14:02,508 --> 00:14:04,431
Higher numbers are considered possible.

236
00:14:04,431 --> 00:14:07,123
At Ocean Beach the beach already narrows

237
00:14:07,123 --> 00:14:09,815
to nothing during the
largest winter swells.

238
00:14:09,815 --> 00:14:12,508
Waves reach the base of the wall

239
00:14:12,508 --> 00:14:16,354
and throw spray over the
top onto the old lanes.

240
00:14:16,354 --> 00:14:20,585
Add more water and the same
storms reach it more often.

241
00:14:20,585 --> 00:14:23,662
Nothing on the west side of the city

242
00:14:23,662 --> 00:14:27,123
is built to a higher
standard than that wall.

243
00:14:27,723 --> 00:14:30,800
California law already
requires sellers to hand buyers

244
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:33,877
a natural hazard disclosure
before a sale closes.

245
00:14:33,877 --> 00:14:37,338
It lists flood zones, fire
zones, and earthquake faults.

246
00:14:37,338 --> 00:14:40,800
Coastal erosion is a
newer entry on that form,

247
00:14:40,800 --> 00:14:45,800
and it does not say how many
winters a given seawall has left.

248
00:14:45,800 --> 00:14:47,723
Insurance follows the same pattern.

249
00:14:47,723 --> 00:14:50,800
A standard homeowner policy
covers wind and fire,

250
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:56,569
and flood coverage from the sea is a
separate federal policy with its own limits.

251
00:14:56,569 --> 00:14:59,262
Several owners along
the coast have learned

252
00:14:59,262 --> 00:15:01,954
that distinction only
after the water arrived.

253
00:15:01,954 --> 00:15:06,954
On the Great Highway row, the house
may be worth three million dollars.

254
00:15:06,954 --> 00:15:10,415
The coverage behind it
is a fraction of that.

255
00:15:11,015 --> 00:15:14,092
The third problem is written into the law.

256
00:15:14,092 --> 00:15:17,169
The Coastal Commission has
become steadily less willing

257
00:15:17,169 --> 00:15:20,246
to approve new hard armoring
anywhere in California.

258
00:15:20,246 --> 00:15:23,708
A wall that protects a house or a road

259
00:15:23,708 --> 00:15:27,554
destroys the public beach
in front of it over time.

260
00:15:27,554 --> 00:15:29,862
The commission's job is the beach.

261
00:15:29,862 --> 00:15:34,862
San Francisco got its rock revetment
south of Sloat only as an emergency.

262
00:15:34,862 --> 00:15:37,938
The price of that permission was a promise

263
00:15:37,938 --> 00:15:41,015
to remove the road and rebuild the beach.

264
00:15:41,015 --> 00:15:44,477
Suppose the owner of a
three million dollar house

265
00:15:44,477 --> 00:15:49,092
on the highway wanted a new
wall in front of the block.

266
00:15:49,092 --> 00:15:51,785
They would be asking the same agency

267
00:15:51,785 --> 00:15:54,862
that just told their own city to retreat.

268
00:15:54,862 --> 00:15:59,862
There is no realistic version of
that request that ends with a permit.

269
00:16:00,462 --> 00:16:03,923
Put those three problems
together and you can see

270
00:16:03,923 --> 00:16:07,769
what the highway actually
was for the people behind it.

271
00:16:07,769 --> 00:16:12,000
It was a buffer between their
front doors and the ocean.

272
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,692
A hundred feet of pavement and plowed

273
00:16:14,692 --> 00:16:17,769
sand absorbed whatever
came off the water first.

274
00:16:17,769 --> 00:16:20,077
That buffer is now a park

275
00:16:20,077 --> 00:16:23,923
in the middle and a
demolition site in the south.

276
00:16:23,923 --> 00:16:28,154
Nobody has proposed knocking down
the houses, and nobody needs to.

277
00:16:28,154 --> 00:16:31,231
The city's own adaptation
plan draws a line

278
00:16:31,231 --> 00:16:34,308
at the sewer tunnel and
the treatment plant.

279
00:16:34,308 --> 00:16:38,154
The houses along the upper
highway sit outside that line,

280
00:16:38,154 --> 00:16:42,385
behind a wall that the plan
does not promise to replace.

281
00:16:42,385 --> 00:16:47,000
Ask a city engineer what happens
to that wall in fifty years

282
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:51,615
and the honest answer is that
nobody has funded the study yet.

283
00:16:51,615 --> 00:16:56,615
The distance between the sea and the
front door has simply gotten shorter.

284
00:16:56,615 --> 00:17:01,231
Every plan the city has published
assumes it will keep getting shorter.

285
00:17:01,831 --> 00:17:06,831
There is one more piece of the
puzzle that most residents never see.

286
00:17:06,831 --> 00:17:09,523
Sand does not only leave Ocean Beach.

287
00:17:09,523 --> 00:17:12,600
Some of it is put back by machine.

288
00:17:12,600 --> 00:17:16,446
The Army Corps of Engineers
regularly dredges the main shipping

289
00:17:16,446 --> 00:17:20,292
channel outside the Golden
Gate to keep the port open.

290
00:17:20,292 --> 00:17:24,138
For years part of that
dredged sand has been placed

291
00:17:24,138 --> 00:17:27,985
offshore of Ocean Beach or
directly on the southern shore.

292
00:17:27,985 --> 00:17:29,908
Barges and pipelines move it,

293
00:17:29,908 --> 00:17:33,369
and bulldozers spread it
along the bluff at Sloat.

294
00:17:33,369 --> 00:17:38,369
It is the same practice used on
beaches in Florida and New Jersey.

295
00:17:38,369 --> 00:17:41,831
It buys time, and it has to be repeated.

296
00:17:41,831 --> 00:17:46,831
A single placement can involve hundreds
of thousands of cubic yards of sand.

297
00:17:46,831 --> 00:17:49,523
Much of it washes away again within

298
00:17:49,523 --> 00:17:52,600
a few storm seasons,
and the barges return.

299
00:17:52,600 --> 00:17:56,062
Every load of sand that
arrives is a reminder.

300
00:17:56,062 --> 00:17:59,138
The beach in its natural state is no

301
00:17:59,138 --> 00:18:02,600
longer wide enough to
protect what sits behind it.

302
00:18:03,200 --> 00:18:06,277
To see how fast the ending can come,

303
00:18:06,277 --> 00:18:08,969
look at the neighbors who already lost.

304
00:18:08,969 --> 00:18:12,431
About fifteen miles south
lies the town of Pacifica.

305
00:18:12,431 --> 00:18:15,892
A row of apartment buildings
on Esplanade Avenue stood

306
00:18:15,892 --> 00:18:20,123
there on a bluff very much
like the one at Sloat.

307
00:18:20,123 --> 00:18:23,200
In the winter of 2016 the same kind

308
00:18:23,200 --> 00:18:27,046
of storms that hit Ocean
Beach in 2010 arrived again.

309
00:18:27,046 --> 00:18:31,662
They cut that bluff back until
the balconies hung over open air.

310
00:18:31,662 --> 00:18:33,969
The city declared the
buildings uninhabitable.

311
00:18:33,969 --> 00:18:37,815
Residents were given hours
to carry out what they could.

312
00:18:37,815 --> 00:18:42,046
Excavators tore the structures down
while news helicopters filmed from above.

313
00:18:42,046 --> 00:18:43,969
Those were ordinary rental apartments.

314
00:18:43,969 --> 00:18:47,046
Their owners had no more power to stop

315
00:18:47,046 --> 00:18:50,508
the ocean than anyone on
the Great Highway does.

316
00:18:50,508 --> 00:18:52,815
Some of them had bought only

317
00:18:52,815 --> 00:18:55,892
a few years earlier at full market price.

318
00:18:55,892 --> 00:18:58,969
A bluff that looked solid on the day

319
00:18:58,969 --> 00:19:02,431
of the inspection was gone
within a single winter.

320
00:19:02,431 --> 00:19:06,662
The difference at Ocean Beach
is only that the sewer tunnel

321
00:19:06,662 --> 00:19:10,892
gave the city a reason to act
before the buildings fell.

322
00:19:11,492 --> 00:19:16,108
Go back further and this neighborhood
has done all of this before.

323
00:19:16,108 --> 00:19:18,800
At the north end of the beach

324
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:21,492
the road climbs toward the Cliff House.

325
00:19:21,492 --> 00:19:26,108
An amusement park called Playland
at the Beach ran there for decades.

326
00:19:26,108 --> 00:19:28,415
It had a wooden roller coaster,

327
00:19:28,415 --> 00:19:33,415
a funhouse with a laughing mechanical
woman at the door, and a carousel.

328
00:19:33,415 --> 00:19:36,877
It closed in 1972 and was
replaced by condominiums.

329
00:19:36,877 --> 00:19:39,569
Just up the hill, the Sutro Baths

330
00:19:39,569 --> 00:19:43,415
were once the largest indoor
swimming complex in the world.

331
00:19:43,415 --> 00:19:46,108
They burned in 1966 and were left

332
00:19:46,108 --> 00:19:49,954
as concrete ruins that fill
with seawater at high tide.

333
00:19:49,954 --> 00:19:53,800
At the south end, the
Fleishhacker Pool beside the zoo

334
00:19:53,800 --> 00:19:58,415
was for years the largest outdoor
swimming pool in the United States.

335
00:19:58,415 --> 00:20:02,262
It was filled with water
pumped straight from the ocean.

336
00:20:02,262 --> 00:20:06,108
It closed in 1971 after storms
damaged the intake pipe,

337
00:20:06,108 --> 00:20:09,569
and the empty basin was
eventually filled with dirt.

338
00:20:09,569 --> 00:20:12,262
Every generation at Ocean Beach has built

339
00:20:12,262 --> 00:20:15,338
something ambitious at
the edge of the water.

340
00:20:15,338 --> 00:20:18,415
Every generation has then
watched the edge move.

341
00:20:19,015 --> 00:20:22,862
What is different this
time is that the city wrote

342
00:20:22,862 --> 00:20:26,708
the retreat into an official
plan and paid for it.

343
00:20:26,708 --> 00:20:31,323
That is the honest answer to the
question this story started with.

344
00:20:31,323 --> 00:20:34,785
San Francisco is losing
the highway to a single

345
00:20:34,785 --> 00:20:38,246
storm or a single election
only on the surface.

346
00:20:38,246 --> 00:20:41,708
Underneath, it is giving
the road up in stages.

347
00:20:41,708 --> 00:20:45,169
A sewer tunnel and a
public beach were judged

348
00:20:45,169 --> 00:20:48,631
to be worth more than
four lanes of asphalt.

349
00:20:48,631 --> 00:20:51,323
And the law will not allow anyone

350
00:20:51,323 --> 00:20:54,015
to hold the line with rock forever.

351
00:20:54,015 --> 00:20:58,631
The homeowners along the road
are left with the view they paid

352
00:20:58,631 --> 00:21:03,631
for and a shrinking strip of sand
between it and their living rooms.

353
00:21:04,231 --> 00:21:09,231
You can buy a house on the Great
Highway for three million dollars.

354
00:21:09,231 --> 00:21:12,692
You can stand at your
upstairs window and watch

355
00:21:12,692 --> 00:21:16,923
the entire Pacific Ocean arrive
in front of you every evening.

356
00:21:16,923 --> 00:21:20,769
You can sweep the sand off
your steps every morning

357
00:21:20,769 --> 00:21:25,000
and repaint the salt off your
garage door every few years.

358
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:31,154
But you cannot get the city to plow
the road that used to catch that sand,

359
00:21:31,154 --> 00:21:33,846
because the road is a park now.

360
00:21:33,846 --> 00:21:37,308
You cannot get the
state to approve a wall,

361
00:21:37,308 --> 00:21:43,077
because the state just spent a decade
convincing your own city to take one down.

362
00:21:43,077 --> 00:21:46,154
And you cannot make the beach stay put.

363
00:21:46,154 --> 00:21:50,769
The sand at Ocean Beach has been
moving since before the seawall,

364
00:21:50,769 --> 00:21:55,769
before the highway, and before
anyone drew a lot line in the dunes.

365
00:21:56,369 --> 00:21:59,062
Which leaves the question that every owner

366
00:21:59,062 --> 00:22:02,138
on that row will
eventually have to answer.

367
00:22:02,138 --> 00:22:07,523
If the city was right to pull its
own highway back from the water,

368
00:22:07,523 --> 00:22:11,369
what does that say about the
houses across the pavement?

369
00:22:11,369 --> 00:22:14,446
Tell me in the comments what you think

370
00:22:14,446 --> 00:22:17,908
San Francisco should do
with the next hundred feet.

371
00:22:17,908 --> 00:22:21,369
Tell me whether you
would sign a thirty year

372
00:22:21,369 --> 00:22:24,831
loan on a house that
faces the open Pacific.

373
00:22:24,831 --> 00:22:27,138
I read every comment on these,

374
00:22:27,138 --> 00:22:31,754
and on this one I genuinely
want to know where you land.
